<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kandahar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kandahar is a city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, long contested between Iranian and Indian powers. Under the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghilzai">Ghilzai Afghans</a> revolted under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirwais-hotak">Mir Wais</a> from 1704. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-hotak">Mir Mahmud Hotak</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in 1722, bringing down the Safavid state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kandahar.jpg" alt="Kandahar" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karla Marshall · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_a_section_of_Kandahar_in_2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kandahar lies between the Arghandab River and the Tarnak River at an elevation of about 1,010 metres. Its position on the main trade route linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> with West Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> made it a frequent target of conquest. The climate is hot and semi-arid, with very dry summers and cool winters. Scholars derive the name probably from Gandhara: early Arab writers often gave Gandhara itself as Kandahar, and the geographer al-Ma'sudi held that Gandharians settled on the banks of the Arghastan and carried the name with them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region is one of the oldest known areas of settlement. Farming villages appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> around 5000 BCE, and the site of Deh Morasi Ghundai lies 27 kilometres south-west of the city. Mundigak, established around 3000 BCE, served as the provincial capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arachosia">Arachosia</a>. Alexander the Great founded what is now Old Kandahar in 330 BCE, near Mundigak, as Alexandria Arachosia. After a brief conflict in 305 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> ceded Arachosia to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a>.</p><p>Arab armies began the conquest of the region in the 7th century; Abbad ibn Ziyad governed Sijistan from 673 to 681. In 870 Yaqub ibn Layth Saffari, a Saffarid ruler, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> the official religion of the area. The Ghaznavids later held the city, which appears to have been renamed Teginabad for a time. The chronicler Minhaj-i-Siraj attributed the fall of the Ghaznavids to their loss of Kandahar.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirwais-hotak">Mirwais Hotak</a>, a chief of the Ghilji, killed the Safavid governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-xi-of-kartli">Gurgin Khan</a>, an ethnic Georgian, and founded the Hotak dynasty in Kandahar. He is said to have resisted Persian attempts to convert the people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> to Shia Islam. He died in November 1715 and was succeeded by his brother Abdul Aziz, who was killed by Mirwais's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-hotak">Mahmud Hotak</a>. In 1747 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> made Kandahar the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a>. The British occupied it briefly in the 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kandahar is the capital of Kandahar Province and has an estimated 732,629 settled residents, most of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtuns</a> who speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashto">Pashto</a>. The municipality is divided into 15 city districts. Its economy rests on agriculture, trade and transport, and the surrounding region is known for grapes and pomegranates.</p><p>The Mausoleum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> stands near the last surviving historical city gate. Opposite it is the Khirka Sharif, which holds a cloak attributed to Muhammad and is brought out only in times of great crisis. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mullah-mohammad-omar">Mullah Omar</a> displayed it in November 1996 to have himself declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Amir al-Mu'minin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kandahar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ahmad Shah Durrani, formerly commander of Nader's Abdali Afghan contingent, was chosen as ruler at <b>Kandahar</b> within months of Nader's death and founded the Durrani Empire that became the basis of modern Afghanistan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>When Nader Shah died in June 1747, Ahmad secured <b>Kandahar</b> and Ghazni, and a loya jirga, a grand council, met near Kandahar for nine days.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1709 the Ghilzai Afghans of <b>Kandahar</b>, Sunnis resentful of Safavid rule, rose under Mir Wais Hotak.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Indian emperor Ashoka, in the 3rd century BCE, addressed the peoples of his north-western frontier in Aramaic, as in his bilingual inscription at <b>Kandahar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/aramaic">Aramaic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Greek of the <b>Kandahar</b> Greek Edict is of a very high level and shows philosophical refinement.</blockquote><a href="/work/edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>The Arghuns, Turko-Mongol nobles who had served the Timurid rulers of Herat, held <b>Kandahar</b> until Babur drove them out in the 1510s.</blockquote><a href="/state/arghun-dynasty">Arghun dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
